          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēversor, dēversārī — \"to lodge, to stay as a guest\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mercātor apud amīcum in oppidō dēversātur. The merchant lodges with a friend in the town."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent: dēversor, dēversārī, dēversātus sum, often with apud 'at the house of'. The inn is a dēversōrium."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēversor is a frequentative built on dēvertō 'to turn aside' (Lewis & Short): to keep turning in, to stay as a guest. dēversōrium 'inn' comes from the same verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
